-8.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.78}, "rating_margin": -15.3, "sigma_race": 6.8, "sigma_total": 7.42, "baseline_margin": -10.35, "analyst_adjustment": -0.15, "key_factors": ["R+8 district with unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings from all ten raters", "Harris holds a massive cash advantage: $1.85M on hand vs $73K for Schwartz", "Only one poll all cycle, a July Democratic-sponsored survey showing Harris +13", "Maryland redistricting push and a Democratic-tilted national climate are tail risks"], "rationale": "The district's eight-point Republican lean, unanimous top-tier ratings, Harris's overwhelming financial edge, and the lone July poll showing him ahead by thirteen points all point to a comfortable incumbent win; a favorable Democratic environment and the unresolved redistricting fight keep this short of Safe.", "watch": "Enactment of a new Maryland map that redraws MD-01, or a late-breaking Democratic wave, would be the clearest sign this race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 68 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Andy Harris (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maryland's 1st District, covering the Eastern Shore and nearby Chesapeake Bay communities, leans Republican by about eight points, and longtime incumbent Andy Harris is seeking another term. Every major race rating published this fall places the seat in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and the only poll of the cycle, a July survey from a Democratic-sponsored firm, showed Harris ahead by thirteen points among likely voters. Harris has also built a large financial advantage, with roughly $1.85 million on hand compared with about $73,000 for Democrat Dan Schwartz.\n\nThe main sources of uncertainty are timing and map politics. Polling is sparse and now months old, prediction markets still give Schwartz a modest chance, and a Maryland redistricting effort discussed over the summer could theoretically redraw the district, though both